# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Leviticus'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'law'
SUBTITLE = 'Holiness, Atonement, and the Worship of Israel’s Camp'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Leviticus is the LORD’s word to Moses for the children of Israel from the tabernacle. It gives the offerings, the consecration of the priests, the laws of clean and unclean, the day of atonement, the laws of holy living, the feasts and sabbaths, the jubile, and the blessings and curses of the covenant (Lev. 1:1; 16:30; 23:2; 25:10; 26:12).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel is God’s covenant nation, set apart from other people, with a priesthood and a tabernacle for its worship (Lev. 20:26; 26:12).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Leviticus', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Offerings (Chs. 1–7)'), ('p', 'The burnt offering, the meat offering, the peace offering, the sin offering and the trespass offering are set out, each with its own ordinance (Lev. 1:4; 4:1–3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Priesthood Consecrated (Chs. 8–10)'), ('p', 'Aaron and his sons are consecrated, the offerings begin, and Nadab and Abihu are judged (Lev. 8:1–36; 10:3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Clean and Unclean (Chs. 11–15)'), ('p', 'Laws on food, birth, leprosy and issues separate the clean from the unclean in Israel’s camp (Lev. 11:44; 13:1–3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Day of Atonement (Ch. 16)'), ('p', 'On one day in the year the priest makes an atonement for the people to cleanse them (Lev. 16:30).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The Holiness Code (Chs. 17–22)'), ('p', 'Laws on blood, on conduct, on neighbour and stranger, and on the priests call Israel to be holy (Lev. 17:11; 19:2, 18; 20:26).'), ('h3', 'Movement 6 — Feasts, Sabbaths, and the Jubile (Chs. 23–25)'), ('p', 'The feasts of the LORD, the sabbath, the sabbath of the land and the jubile are given (Lev. 23:2–3; 25:10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 7 — Blessings, Curses, and the Covenant (Chs. 26–27)'), ('p', 'The LORD sets out blessings for obedience and curses for disobedience, and says He will not break His covenant with them (Lev. 26:12, 44).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Leviticus', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Atonement by Offering</b> — The offerer lays his hand on the head of the burnt offering, “and it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him” (Lev. 1:4).', '<b>The Priesthood</b> — Aaron and his sons are consecrated to minister at the tabernacle (Lev. 8:1–36).', '<b>Holiness</b> — “Ye shall be holy: for I the LORD your God am holy” (Lev. 19:2).', '<b>The Blood</b> — “It is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul” (Lev. 17:11).', '<b>The Day of Atonement</b> — “On that day shall the priest make an atonement for you, to cleanse you” (Lev. 16:30).', '<b>The Feasts of the LORD</b> — “Concerning the feasts of the LORD, which ye shall proclaim to be holy convocations, even these are my feasts” (Lev. 23:2).', '<b>The Jubile</b> — “Ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land” (Lev. 25:10).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Leviticus', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Leviticus points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Offering</b> — The offerings of Leviticus point to the one offering of Christ (Lev. 1:4; Heb. 10:1–10).', '<b>The Great High Priest</b> — Aaron’s priesthood anticipates Christ as Israel’s great high priest (Lev. 8:1–12; Heb. 4:14–15).', '<b>The Blood and Atonement</b> — The blood that makes atonement for the soul is the pattern the New Testament uses of Christ’s blood (Lev. 17:11; Heb. 9:11–14).', '<b>The Day of Atonement</b> — The yearly atonement for Israel is the background of Hebrews’ account of Christ’s entrance (Lev. 16:30; Heb. 9:24–28).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Leviticus', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Leviticus speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Leviticus operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Leviticus in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Leviticus reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['covenant separation from other people (Lev. 20:26)', 'priesthood and tabernacle worship (Lev. 8:1–12)', 'sacrificial system (Lev. 1:4; 17:11)', 'feasts, sabbaths and jubile (Lev. 23:2–3; 25:10)', 'covenant with its blessings and curses (Lev. 26:12, 44)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Leviticus that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Leviticus contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Burnt Offering — “it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him.” (Lev. 1:4).', 'Holy Because He Is Holy — “ye shall therefore sanctify yourselves, and ye shall be holy; for I am holy.” (Lev. 11:44).', 'The Day of Atonement — “on that day shall the priest make an atonement for you, to cleanse you.” (Lev. 16:30).', 'The Blood — “the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar.” (Lev. 17:11).', 'A Holy Congregation — “Speak unto all the congregation of the children of Israel, and say unto them, Ye shall be holy.” (Lev. 19:2).', 'Severed from Other People — “have severed you from other people, that ye should be mine.” (Lev. 20:26).', 'The Feasts of the LORD — “Concerning the feasts of the LORD, which ye shall proclaim to be holy convocations.” (Lev. 23:2).', 'The Sabbath of Rest — “the seventh day is the sabbath of rest, an holy convocation.” (Lev. 23:3).', 'The Jubile — “it shall be a jubile unto you.” (Lev. 25:10).', 'The Covenant Held — “I will walk among you, and will be your God, and ye shall be my people.” (Lev. 26:12).', 'The Covenant Not Broken — “I will not cast them away, neither will I abhor them, to destroy them utterly.” (Lev. 26:44).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Leviticus Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Leviticus and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Leviticus teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Leviticus', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Burnt Offering', '“it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 1:4'], ['Holy Because He Is Holy', '“ye shall therefore sanctify yourselves, and ye shall be holy; for I am holy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 11:44'], ['The Day of Atonement', '“on that day shall the priest make an atonement for you, to cleanse you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 16:30'], ['The Blood', '“the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 17:11'], ['A Holy Congregation', '“Speak unto all the congregation of the children of Israel, and say unto them, Ye shall be holy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 19:2'], ['Severed from Other People', '“have severed you from other people, that ye should be mine.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 20:26'], ['The Feasts of the LORD', '“Concerning the feasts of the LORD, which ye shall proclaim to be holy convocations.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 23:2'], ['The Sabbath of Rest', '“the seventh day is the sabbath of rest, an holy convocation.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 23:3'], ['The Jubile', '“it shall be a jubile unto you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 25:10'], ['The Covenant Held', '“I will walk among you, and will be your God, and ye shall be my people.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 26:12'], ['The Covenant Not Broken', '“I will not cast them away, neither will I abhor them, to destroy them utterly.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Lev. 26:44']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Leviticus that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Leviticus teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The Holiness of God', '“for I the LORD your God am holy.”', 'Lev. 19:2'], ['Honest Dealing', '“Thou shalt not defraud thy neighbour, neither rob him.”', 'Lev. 19:13'], ['Love of Neighbour', '“thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.”', 'Lev. 19:18'], ['Honour for the Aged', '“Thou shalt rise up before the hoary head, and honour the face of the old man.”', 'Lev. 19:32'], ['Just Measures', '“Just balances, just weights, a just ephah, and a just hin, shall ye have.”', 'Lev. 19:36']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Leviticus without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Leviticus in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Honour the holiness of God</b> — He is the LORD, and He is holy (Lev. 19:2).', '<b>Deal honestly</b> — Do not defraud or rob (Lev. 19:13).', '<b>Love your neighbour</b> — As yourself (Lev. 19:18).', '<b>Honour the aged</b> — Rise before the hoary head (Lev. 19:32).', '<b>Keep just measures</b> — Use just balances and weights (Lev. 19:36).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Leviticus', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Leviticus in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Offerings (Chs. 1–7) — Atonement at the altar. <i>(Lev. 1:4)</i>', 'The Priesthood (Chs. 8–10) — Aaron and his sons consecrated. <i>(Lev. 8:1–12; 10:3)</i>', 'Clean and Unclean (Chs. 11–15) — The camp set apart. <i>(Lev. 11:44)</i>', 'The Day of Atonement (Ch. 16) — The yearly cleansing. <i>(Lev. 16:30)</i>', 'The Holiness Code (Chs. 17–22) — Blood, neighbour, stranger and priests. <i>(Lev. 17:11; 19:2, 18; 20:26)</i>', 'Feasts, Sabbaths, and the Jubile (Chs. 23–25) — The appointed times. <i>(Lev. 23:2–3; 25:10)</i>', 'Blessings and Curses (Chs. 26–27) — The covenant held. <i>(Lev. 26:12, 44)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Leviticus, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward devotion, worship, and humility without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Leviticus simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Leviticus stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
